## Inline code {#inline-code}

**Inline code** inserts a computed value into prose. Put it in a sentence:

``` markdown
We measured `{{r}} nrow(penguins)` penguins.
```

. . .

It renders with the live count: "We measured **342** penguins."

. . .

The number updates when the data changes.

. . .

- Put the engine name in the braces. `{{python}}` and `{{julia}}` use the same syntax.
- Keep inline expressions short. Compute complex values in a cell first, then insert one result.
- Inline code is not evaluated in document metadata such as `title`, or in cell options such as
  `#| fig-cap`. Use `!expr` for cell options.
- Inline results are escaped as text. In R, wrap a value in `I()` to preserve its Markdown
  formatting.

**Learn more:** [Inline code](https://quarto.org/docs/computations/inline-code.html)

::: notes
- **Do:** show inline `nrow(penguins)`.
- **Say:** "The number updates with the data."
- **Watch for:** a cell-option caption shows inline-code syntax as literal text instead of
  evaluating it.
:::

## Layouts: body, margin, and beyond {#layouts}

::: {.callout-warning title="Eyes up — not a live *Follow along*"}
The live build is done. The next slides cover layout concepts. Your document is ready for the
Challenge.
